#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210567c7-e894-5b02-9ee8-c6893172df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425735} {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20eb7d-1ae8-5c45-ab85-1bae5f9c5811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.32\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425747} {:answer/id #uuid "cce28df6-b4e2-5293-9860-139a95e35caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425748} {:answer/id #uuid "cfed1752-e352-5a6b-9e2d-7dc6a36ff6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.33\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ca134-e512-533c-83e5-e06d13cf4c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcb58811-e371-5576-bf66-5ecfddbfc1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143202" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm}\\,10\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(20+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {5}^{2}+{10}^{2})-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(200)-36 \\\\  & =40-36 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}≈1.15\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d27b381-2583-5b7a-9fca-f1b985469a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490564} {:answer/id #uuid "285d902e-e585-5afb-b7d0-a096327f6bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490584} {:answer/id #uuid "720b4a42-214e-5e9a-9503-6877dcdc5e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490736} {:answer/id #uuid "88c5126e-12bf-54b2-b915-69e90cd55a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490783} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaa20be-1127-571b-9b08-97a73a594545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "720b4a42-214e-5e9a-9503-6877dcdc5e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490736} :answer-field/id #uuid "a928408c-e2bb-5ac3-8926-8be8d60e417f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbae1413-e0da-5920-9a81-9f35b978911e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143422" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ is a sample of size $n=50$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =12$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=4$ and the sample size is $n=50,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{4}{50} \\\\  & =0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.08}≈0.28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb4622f-90b6-5a96-a333-ac1ffe1c4ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490568} {:answer/id #uuid "7156e35a-a0cd-5c4a-9cfe-86803b267024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490734} {:answer/id #uuid "cad96efa-207b-59ed-9afb-6c27a870f1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,305" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490895} {:answer/id #uuid "db47dba0-0807-5e27-ac1c-6bd558d9926c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490927} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1775bd-6de6-57e4-b281-5dc5531234f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc1775bd-6de6-57e4-b281-5dc5531234f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490928} :answer-field/id #uuid "a38d7fe4-d3d9-5f07-8a46-a68180a20afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df86b42c-6e72-556f-b425-3d68c35d4d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143440" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =24$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{1}=1,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >2 \\\\ n & >{2}^{2} \\\\ n & >4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2340fb78-3bc0-52f2-8490-70b953ad6bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490575} {:answer/id #uuid "5361feae-c64c-5ad9-9e88-dc19f7cade4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490670} {:answer/id #uuid "78e50466-a068-54f3-a9e1-1405af2707ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490751} {:answer/id #uuid "96643632-e50d-5725-b254-61e0b31e5d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490816} {:answer/id #uuid "e63fe34a-b9f7-5470-aaa5-176d56673f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2340fb78-3bc0-52f2-8490-70b953ad6bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490575} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aee0077-edc2-5b86-802c-50ab5c7a8951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f4e58b8-0514-5aa4-9211-b553375a01af" :question/math-academy-id "q-143489" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ coins is drawn from a population. The population has coins of two different denominations, $$1$ and $$0.50,$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $$0.02.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $$1$ and $$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00419cc9-1483-5e06-b0b3-661e4e54899f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490512} {:answer/id #uuid "47723024-b4a1-584b-a829-85dceb9329c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490643} {:answer/id #uuid "80b0e3c0-0da7-58eb-94d5-d78a9853d5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490770} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb60d40-47ca-586a-82af-614639d331fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490897} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c0630-b233-59ad-b582-a2188863f6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb60d40-47ca-586a-82af-614639d331fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490897} :answer-field/id #uuid "c686f557-e13f-5945-8efd-5e2a53507af7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18073722-b26c-57f1-b864-bfe7c648363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143495" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ shirts is drawn from a population. The population has shirts of two different sizes, $8$ and $10,$ distributed in the ratio $1:3.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the shirts in the population have sizes $8$ and $10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+3=4$ shirts only with the following sizes:\n\n$$\n8,\\,10,\\,10,\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+3\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+30) \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{4} \\\\  & =9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({8}^{2}+3\\cdot {10}^{2})-{9.5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(364)-90.25 \\\\  & =91-90.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.75},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.75}}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{{0.5}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{0.25}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=4.$"}]}
